@prefix bao:  <https://w3id.org/boolean-algebra-ontology#>.
@prefix rdf:  <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#>.
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#>.
@prefix sh:   <http://www.w3.org/ns/shacl#>.
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix :   <http://www.example.org#>.


# The triples below represent the following assertions: {(¬a∨b), (a∨¬c), ¬b, c}, where:
# - "a" means "Bill's wife is Mary".
# - "b" means "Bill's wife is Sue".
# - "c" means "Bill's wife is Jenny".
# The rules of the Boolean Algebra Ontology derive all pairs of contradictory literals from the above knowledge in two steps:
# (1) First, Unit Propagation is applied to "(¬a∨b)" and "¬b", from which "¬a" is inferred. At the same time, Unit Propagation is applied to "(a∨¬c)" and "c", from which "a" is inferred.
# (2) Then, Unit Propagation is applied to "(¬a∨b)" and "a" (which was inferred in step (1)), from which "b" is inferred. Simultaneously, Unit Propagation is applied to "(a∨¬c)" and "¬a" (which was also inferred in step (1)), from which "¬c" is inferred.
# Since the knowledge graph now contains "a" and "¬a", "b" and "¬b", and "c" and "¬c", contradictions are derived for all three pairs of complementary literals.

# "(¬a∨b)": Bill's wife is not Mary or she is Sue.
[a bao:Disjunction;
	bao:has-disjunct << <<:Bill :hasWife :Mary>> rdf:type bao:False>>;
	bao:has-disjunct << <<:Bill :hasWife :Sue>> rdf:type bao:True>>].

# "(a∨¬c)": Bill's wife is Mary or she is not Jenny.
[a bao:Disjunction;
	bao:has-disjunct << <<:Bill :hasWife :Mary>> rdf:type bao:True>>;
	bao:has-disjunct << <<:Bill :hasWife :Jenny>> rdf:type bao:False>>].

# "¬b": Bill's wife is not Sue.
<<:Bill :hasWife :Sue>> rdf:type bao:False.

# "c": Bill's wife is Jenny.
:Bill :hasWife :Jenny.
